course: biology
lesson: 3
title: The rainforest from below: understorey and forest floor
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Under the canopy lies the understorey, a dim world of young trees, shrubs, vines and big-leaved plants, and below that the forest floor, where only about 2 per cent of the sunlight arrives.
explain: It is so dark at midday that a camera needs a torch, and because so little light gets through, the floor is often surprisingly easy to walk across, except where a giant tree has fallen and let the sun in.
explain: The floor is the forest's kitchen and recycling bin: fungi, bacteria, termites and worms turn a fallen leaf into food for the trees again in just weeks, so the soil itself stays thin and poor.
explain: Down here animals hide, warn or surprise: a frog looks exactly like a dead leaf, a tiny red frog shouts «poison!» with its colours, and leafcutter ants march past like a line of little green sails.
explain: In cartoons a centipede always has exactly 100 legs and wears 100 shoes; a real centipede never has exactly 100 legs, because it always has an odd number of pairs, such as 15, 21 or 23 pairs.
fact: Only about 2 per cent of sunlight reaches the floor of a thick rainforest, so few plants grow there except where a big tree has fallen.
fact: Jaguars rest in trees, swim well and hunt capybaras, caimans, turtles and fish; they are the only big cats in the Americas and have the strongest bite for their size of any big cat.
fact: Leafcutter ants do not eat the leaves they carry; they chew them into a mulch to grow a fungus garden, and the fungus feeds a colony that can hold several million ants.
fact: Poison dart frogs warn predators with bright colours, and their poison comes mainly from the ants and mites they eat, so zoo-raised frogs fed on fruit flies are not poisonous.
fact: Rainforest soil is thin, so roots stay near the surface, and many tall trees grow wide buttress roots that prop them up like the walls of a tent.
fact: Tapirs are shy plant-eaters with a short flexible snout that grabs leaves and can work as a snorkel; their closest relatives are horses and rhinos, not pigs.
fact: The Malayan leaf frog is brown and flat with pointed folds above its eyes, so on a floor of dead leaves it looks just like one of them.
fact: On the warm, wet floor fungi, bacteria, termites and worms break dead leaves down in weeks, and the trees take the nutrients straight back, which is why cleared rainforest land often becomes poor within a few years of farming.
fact: The okapi of the Congo rainforest is the giraffe's closest living relative, has zebra-like stripes on its legs and a long tongue that can reach its own ears.
fact: Giant tropical centipedes hunt insects, spiders and even small frogs and mice at night; their first pair of legs are hollow venom fangs, so they must never be handled.
fact: Centipedes always have an odd number of pairs of legs, so no centipede has exactly 100 legs.
fact: The southern cassowary of northern Australia and New Guinea is a huge flightless bird with a casque on its head and a dagger-like inner claw about 10 cm long; it can be dangerous to people who get close.
fact: Cassowaries swallow fruit whole and spread the seeds of over 200 kinds of plants in their droppings.
fact: Giant African land snails can grow shells about 20 cm long and are a serious pest outside Africa, so keeping or releasing them is banned in places like the USA.
exercise: How much sunlight reaches the floor of a thick rainforest? | About 2 per cent | About half of the sunlight | About a quarter of it | All of it, through the gaps
exercise: Why is the rainforest floor often easy to walk across? | Too little light gets through for thick undergrowth to grow | Animals eat every single plant on the floor as soon as it sprouts | People sweep paths through every forest | The floor is flooded and plants cannot grow
exercise: Which big cat lives in the American rainforests and loves water? | The jaguar | The leopard | The tiger | The cheetah
exercise: What do leafcutter ants do with the leaves they carry? | Grow a fungus garden on them, and eat the fungus | Eat the leaves straight away, as soon as they reach the nest | Build the walls of their nest with them | Use them as little umbrellas against the heavy rain
exercise: Why is a poison dart frog so brightly coloured? | To warn predators that it is poisonous | To attract the birds that clean its skin | Because it eats lots of red and blue berries | To soak up more heat in the warm sunshine
exercise: Where does a wild poison dart frog get most of its poison? | From the ants and mites it eats | From the leaves it sleeps on at night | It makes all of it from sunlight | From the rainwater it drinks each day
exercise: What job do buttress roots do? | They prop up tall trees growing in thin soil | They suck water from deep under the ground, far below the river | They catch insects for the tree to eat | They store fruit for the long dry season
exercise: How does a tapir use its short trunk in a stream? | Like a snorkel, while it hides from jaguars | To spray water at its enemies on the bank | To catch fish like a net | To dig deep holes in the riverbed for its food
exercise: Which animals are the tapir's closest relatives? | Horses and rhinos | Pigs and wild boar | Elephants and mammoths | Hippos and dairy cows
exercise: How does the Malayan leaf frog hide? | It looks like a dead leaf on the forest floor | It changes colour to match any background, like a chameleon | It buries itself deep in the mud | It hides inside fallen fruit
exercise: Why does a dead leaf disappear so fast on the rainforest floor? | Heat and wet help fungi, termites and worms break it down | The heavy rain washes it away into the rivers within an hour | Monkeys collect the leaves for nests | The sun burns the leaves away
exercise: Why does cleared rainforest land often become poor for farming? | Most nutrients were in the living plants, not in the thin soil | It rains too little after the trees are cut, so the crops dry up | The farmers forget to plant seeds | The soil turns into sand straight away, as soon as the trees are gone
exercise: Which animal is the okapi's closest living relative? | The giraffe | The plains zebra | The wild horse | The antelope
exercise: Why should you never pick up a giant centipede? | Its first legs are fangs that inject a painful venom | Its hairs stick in your skin like a caterpillar's | It sticks to your skin for ever | It is so fragile it breaks in half if you lift it at all
exercise: Which is true about a cassowary? | It is a huge flightless bird with a dagger-like claw | It is a small green parrot that can talk like a person | It is a bird that flies above the canopy | It is a lizard with a helmet
exercise: How do cassowaries help the forest? | They spread the seeds of over 200 kinds of plants in their droppings | They dig ponds for frogs to live in | They eat all the harmful insects on the floor, so the young trees can grow up safe | They knock down old trees to make space
exercise: Why is keeping giant African land snails banned in places like the USA? | Outside Africa they spread and become serious crop pests | They are too slow and boring to look after properly | They are poisonous to touch | They always escape from their tanks and bite people
test: «centipede means 100 legs so it's got 100, duh» What is right? | No, centipedes always have an odd number of pairs, so never exactly 100 | Yes, every centipede has exactly 100, that is why it has the name | Yes, 50 on each side | No, they all have exactly 1,000
test: Count a centipede with 21 pairs of legs. How many legs is that? | 42 | 21 | 100 | 40
test: A zoo frog of the same kind as a deadly wild poison dart frog has been fed only on fruit flies. Why is it not poisonous? | It does not get the ants and mites that its poison comes from | Zoo frogs lose their colour and their poison | The keepers wash the poison off its skin each morning with warm water | Poison dart frogs are never poisonous at all
test: «wicked, a red frog, gonna pick it up» Which is the best answer? | Don't, never touch a wild brightly coloured frog | Fine, all frogs are safe to hold | Fine, just wash your hands well afterwards with soap | Only touch it with one finger
test: What would happen to a leafcutter colony if all its fungus died? | The colony would go hungry, because the fungus is its food | Nothing, the ants would just eat the leaves | The ants would start eating soil | The queen would grow new fungus from her wings and feed the whole colony
test: «a tapir's just a pig with a nose, innit» What is right? | No, its closest relatives are horses and rhinos | Yes, it is a wild pig with a long nose | Yes, it is a baby elephant | No, it is a kind of anteater
test: «they chopped the jungle and the farm went rubbish in 3 years, why??» Which is the best answer? | The nutrients were in the trees, and the thin soil soon ran out | The farmers used the wrong tractor | The rain stopped for ever after the trees were cut down | The farm was cursed by the forest spirits
test: «okapi's a zebra cos stripes» What is right? | No, it is the giraffe's closest relative, with stripes only on its legs | Yes, it is a zebra that lives in forests | Yes, a zebra crossed with a horse | No, it is a kind of deer
test: Kai's sister says a cassowary is just a big fluffy bird and she wants a selfie with it. What is the best answer? | Keep well away: its dagger-like claw can badly hurt people | It is safe, because flightless birds never fight anyone | Fine, if she offers it some bread | Only run past it quickly
test: «why's the ground so bare under all them massive trees?» Which is the best answer? | The canopy takes almost all the light, so little can grow below | Animals eat everything that grows there | The floor is too wet to grow anything | Trees poison the ground under them so that no other plant can ever grow there
test: «how does a jaguar kill a turtle, the shell's well hard» Which is closest to the truth? | Its very strong bite can break through the shell | It waits for the turtle to come out of its shell | It rolls the turtle into a river | Jaguars never eat turtles
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the illumination of the rainforest floor? | Approximately two per cent of incident sunlight penetrates to the floor of a dense rainforest | The floor of the rainforest receives the entirety of incident sunlight through gaps in the foliage | Approximately one half of incident sunlight reaches the floor | No sunlight whatsoever reaches the floor at any location
exam: Identify the source of the food upon which a colony of leafcutter ants subsists. | A fungus cultivated upon chewed leaf material within the nest | The leaves themselves, consumed immediately upon collection by the workers | The sap of the trees from which the leaves are cut | Other insects captured upon the forest floor
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the toxicity of poison dart frogs. | The toxins are derived principally from ants and mites consumed in the wild, wherefore captive-reared specimens are not toxic | The toxins are manufactured entirely from sunlight | All specimens are equally toxic irrespective of their diet, whether reared in the wild or maintained in captivity by keepers | No species of poison dart frog possesses any toxin
exam: Ascertain the number of legs possessed by a centipede bearing twenty-three pairs. | Forty-six | Twenty-three | One hundred | Forty-four
